**Why this route works** – It strings together Barcelona’s most‑photographed streets and historic squares, letting you soak up Roman, medieval, and modern life while staying within a compact walking radius. Food stops at La Boqueria and the tapas bars of El Born keep energy levels high.

**Practical tips**

| Tip | Details |
|-----|---------|
| **Tickets** | Reserve the **Picasso Museum** and **Cathedral rooftop** tickets in advance (especially in summer). |
| **Best time** | Early morning for La Rambla (fewer crowds) and late afternoon for the Gothic Quarter (cooler, beautiful light). |
| **Free‑cancellation** | Most tickets (Boqueria food tours, museum entries) are flagged “free cancellation”. |
| **Dining** | Try “pa amb tomàquet” (bread with tomato) at any café, and “botifarra” (Catalan sausage) in the Gothic Quarter. |
| **Dress code** | For the Cathedral and churches, shoulders and knees must be covered; otherwise you may be refused entry. |

